use std
into dial.amni
lang amni

Amni Dial: a GPU coding console that drives four coding-agent CLIs, one wheel sector per session, with a shader-rendered black hole that swallows old output. Imports os, gfx, math, json, `gfx.codec_png`, `gfx.sdf_font` (`sdf_system_font()`, `sdf_text`, `sdf_text_w`, `sdf_rect(vs, x, y, w, h, color)` for a flat rectangle, `sdf_draw`, `TextVtx` with pos, uv, col). Use these std calls and no others for the same jobs (never `extern` and never a hand-written JSON parser): `run_command(cmd)` returns the shell exit status; `is_dir(path)`, `file_exists(path)`; `read_file(path, out)` returns false when missing; `write_file(path, text)`, `append_file(path, text)`; `env(name, out)` returns false when unset; `args()`; `sleep_ms(ms)`; `split(text, byte)`; `str(...)` joins text and numbers; `copy`; `print`. JSON: one reused `json::Doc {}`; `let root, msg = json::parse(text, doc)` gives a negative root on failure; `json::member(doc, node, key)`, `json::element(doc, node, k)`, `json::count(doc, node)`, `json::kind(doc, node)` compared with `json::K_STR`, `json::text_of(doc, node)`, `let v, ok = json::as_f64(doc, node)`, `let b, ok = json::as_bool(doc, node)`, `json::format_string(out, text)`; a missing member is a negative node. Text is a growable byte list (empty is `[]`); a session's lines are a list of them; colors are 4-byte arrays like `TextVtx.col`. Floats are f32 unless stated; NOW, T0 and the spin duration are real. Platforms p from 0 to 3, in this order: name "Grok Bot", "Claude Code", "Antigravity", "OpenCode"; key "grok", "claude", "antigravity", "opencode"; executable "grok", "claude", "agy", "opencode". ENVP is the shell prefix `PATH="$HOME/.local/bin:$HOME/.opencode/bin:$HOME/.agy/bin:$HOME/.grok/bin:$PATH"; ` (ending in a space). RAD = 0.017453292, SPIN = 0.7 seconds.

A session holds its platform, number n, directory, session id text, working directory text, model text, permission mode text, activity text, display lines, a last-seen size (start -1), a shown-line count, busy flag, busy start time (real, -1 when unknown), demo flag, and reals context tokens, cost, output tokens and an int turn count (all 0). Module state: sessions list S, availability per platform AV, last-visited session per platform LASTP, ROOT, HOME text, CUR, ROT, ROT0, ROT1 (all 0), T0 = -9, NOW, SCROLL, VIEW (int, whole lines scrolled back), VIEWT (scroll target in lines) and VIEWF (eased scroll position in lines), both f32 0, HIDE, INPUT text, DARK true, FRAMES false, W = 1400, H = 900, CX = 700, CY = 450, R = 400, WRAPU = 40, YS = 1, BX, BY, WG = 0, S0 = 27, FR = 0, font metrics FY0 = 0, FY1 = 1 and FC = 0.5, LOGO (a vertex list built once at startup), GPU state READY false, four pipelines PB, PO, PD, PU, a sampler SM, a scene texture SC, four bloom textures BL, their size TW = 0 and TH = 0, and up to two holes HX (each four floats x, y, radius, alpha) with count NH. Two uniform structs: `HoleU` with four 4-float vectors a, h0, h1, m and `PostU` with two 4-float vectors a, b.

Colors. A float 0 to 1 becomes a byte as `clamp(x*255, 0, 255)` cut to int. rgba(c, a) packs c's three channels and a. Theme color k with alpha a, dark then light: k0 (0.012,0.018,0.04) / (0.958,0.938,0.882); k1 (0.90,0.88,0.82) / (0.20,0.16,0.09); k2 (1.0,0.80,0.42) / (0.70,0.50,0.12); k3 (0.55,0.58,0.68) / (0.48,0.42,0.32); k4 (0.03,0.045,0.09) / (0.995,0.98,0.94); otherwise (0,0,0) / (0.80,0.68,0.42). Accent color k with alpha a, dark then light: k0 code (0.55,0.90,0.95) / (0.10,0.42,0.50); k1 tool (0.47,0.80,0.98) / (0.17,0.38,0.62); k2 error (1.0,0.40,0.33) / (0.75,0.17,0.12); k3 live (0.40,0.95,0.55) / (0.15,0.55,0.25); k4 busy (1.0,0.72,0.25) / (0.78,0.47,0.05); otherwise demo (0.72,0.55,1.0) / (0.45,0.30,0.75). Platform color: p0 (0.55,0.57,0.65), p1 (0.85,0.50,0.32), p2 (0.32,0.55,0.95), p3 (0.40,0.75,0.45). Shadow color for alpha a: theme 5 with alpha a times 0.45 when dark, 0.12 when light.

Sessions. A session's directory is "ROOT/key/n". Adding one appends it with demo set when "dir/demo" exists, empty cwd and model, busy start -1, and returns its index. Making one for p takes the lowest n from 1 whose directory does not exist, runs `mkdir -p '<dir>'` and adds it. Scanning: for each platform add every existing n from 1 while its directory exists, making one if none existed; then LASTP[p] is the first session index of p (or -1). A platform is available when `command -v <exe> >/dev/null 2>&1`, prefixed by ENVP, exits 0. Tilde form of a path: when it starts with HOME, "~" followed by the rest, else the path.

Navigation. rank(i) counts earlier sessions of i's platform; nth(p, r) is the index of p's session of rank r (or -1); cnt(p) counts p's sessions. Span is 360 / session count degrees; order(i) is rank(i) plus the sessions of a lower platform; a session's center is (order + 0.5) * span. ang(t) = (t + ROT + 90) * RAD. Wrapping an angle in degrees subtracts 360 while above 180 and adds 360 while below -180. Going to session i: d = wrap(-center(i) - ROT1); ROT0 = ROT; ROT1 becomes ROT1 + d; T0 = NOW; CUR = i; LASTP of its platform = i; VIEW = 0, VIEWT = 0 and VIEWF = 0; its shown count = visible(i). Visible lines are max(0, lines - HIDE) for CUR, else all lines. Stepping by dir goes to nth(p, (rank(CUR) + dir + m) % m) for CUR's platform p with m sessions of it.

Display lines. Every line's first byte is its kind letter: "u" user prompt, "t" tool call, "e" error, "c" code, "h" heading level 1, "g" heading level 2, "j" heading level 3 or deeper, "r" horizontal rule, "b" table row, "H" table header row, "m" meta, "q" quote, "k" thinking, "M" display math, "p" plain. A line holding only "p" is a blank line. After the kind letter comes the display text, where marker bytes toggle inline styles: byte 1 code, byte 2 bold, byte 3 italic, byte 4 strikethrough, byte 5 math, byte 6 link; in table rows byte 9 separates cells. The visible width of text at size s sums `sdf_text_w` at size s of each run between marker bytes 1 to 6 and 9, ignoring the markers.

Wrapping `wrap(out, k, t)` appends the display text t as one or more lines of kind k. Walk t by UTF-8 lead byte (at least 240 is 4 bytes, 224 is 3, 192 is 2, else 1), taking the clipped bytes as the character. Skip "\r", 4-byte characters and stray continuation bytes (128 to 191). A tab becomes two spaces, except byte 9 in kinds "b" and "H", which is kept. Bytes 1 to 8, and byte 9 when kept, add no width. The hanging indent h is 0 except in kind "p": there it is the count of leading spaces of t, plus 2 when those spaces are followed by "• ", "◦ ", "▪ ", "☐ " or "☑ ", or plus 2 more than the digit count when they are followed by byte 2, digits, "." and byte 2. The current line starts as the kind letter; after each other character add its `sdf_text_w` at size 1 to the width. In kinds "b", "H" and "t", once the width exceeds WRAPU, append "…" and ignore the rest of t. Otherwise, when the width exceeds WRAPU, find the last space after position h + 1 searching back from the end, cut there if that position exceeds len/3 (integer) else at the full length, push the part before the cut, and start the next line with the kind letter, then h spaces, then for each marker byte from 1 to 8 in order that byte when an odd number of it sits in the pushed part, then the part after the cut skipping one byte; the width becomes the visible width at size 1 of the new line after its kind letter. At the end push the current line when it holds more than the kind letter.

`TexSym` holds name and sym (both text). `texsyms()` gives the table below; build it once into a module var on first use. A character's superscript is the character at the same character position (counting UTF-8 characters, not bytes) in "⁰¹²³⁴⁵⁶⁷⁸⁹⁺⁻⁼⁽⁾ᵃᵇᶜᵈᵉᶠᵍʰⁱʲᵏˡᵐⁿᵒᵖʳˢᵗᵘᵛʷˣʸᶻᵀ′" as it has in "0123456789+−=()abcdefghijklmnoprstuvwxyzT′", and none when it is not there; its subscript likewise maps "0123456789+−=()aehijklmnoprstuvx" to "₀₁₂₃₄₅₆₇₈₉₊₋₌₍₎ₐₑₕᵢⱼₖₗₘₙₒₚᵣₛₜᵤᵥₓ". TeX form `tex(s)` turns LaTeX math into Unicode text. A group read at position j skips spaces, then is the text inside matched braces when a "{" comes next, else the single UTF-8 character there. Walk s: a backslash followed by letters is a command with that name. "frac" reads two groups a and b with tex forms fa and fb and appends fa + "⁄" + fb when both are one character long, else fa (in parentheses when longer than one character), "/", then fb (in parentheses when longer than one character). "sqrt" reads a group g and appends "√" + tex(g) when that form is one character long, else "√(" + tex(g) + ")". "mathbb" reads a group and appends "ℝ", "ℕ", "ℤ", "ℚ" or "ℂ" for "R", "N", "Z", "Q", "C", else the group. "text", "mathrm", "mathbf", "mathit", "mathsf", "mathtt", "boldsymbol" and "operatorname" read a group and append its tex form ("text" appends it unchanged). "left", "right", "displaystyle", "limits", "nonumber", "big", "Big", "bigg" and "Bigg" append nothing. "quad" appends two spaces and "qquad" four. Other names append their sym from texsyms when listed, else the name itself. A backslash followed by any other byte: "\\" , "\," , "\;" , "\:" and "\ " append a space unless the text so far ends with a space or the next byte of s is a space, "\!" nothing, any other byte appends that byte. "^" reads a group, takes its tex form f, and appends f with every character replaced by its superscript when all of them have one, else byte 7 + f + byte 7 (a raised run); "_" does the same with subscripts and byte 8 (a lowered run). "{" and "}" are dropped, "~" becomes a space, "-" becomes "−", "'" becomes "′", a space is skipped when the text so far ends with a space, and every other byte is copied.

